# NOTHING UNDER data/ MAY BE TEXT-MERGED BY GIT.
#
# WHY. `data/` holds files that are BOTH tracked in git AND rewritten by cron on
# the production box. When a deploy pulls a commit touching one that is also dirty
# on the server, git runs its line-based text merge and writes `<<<<<<<` markers
# INSIDE the file. That corrupted production twice in one day (2026-08-23):
#
#   data/rejected-keywords.json   → 20 conflict markers in a file 9 agents parse
#   five data/posts/*/meta.json   → all five invalid JSON on the box
#
# Every reader in this fleet `catch {}`s a parse failure and carries on as though
# the data were EMPTY, so the corruption is SILENT. CLAUDE.md names the root cause
# exactly: "Running a text merge over a machine-written data file is the bug" —
# and no care taken while resolving the conflict prevents it, because the damage
# happens before anyone sees it.
#
# WHAT `-merge` DOES. Git keeps OUR version and declares a conflict for a human to
# resolve. The conflict is still REPORTED (`UU` in git status) — nothing is hidden
# — but no markers are ever written into the file, so the box keeps running on
# valid data while the deploy waits. Verified both ways: without this the JSON
# fails to parse; with it the file is byte-intact and git still reports a conflict.
#
# WHY THE WHOLE DIRECTORY, not a list of the risky paths. A list of subdirectories
# has to be updated by everyone who adds a tracked data file, and it loses its
# protection SILENTLY when they forget — the same failure shape as the meta.json
# "allowlist of what to keep" that quietly destroyed 23 fields per redraft. One
# broad rule fails safe. Durable config lives in config/, not here, and an auto
# text-merge of generated data was never semantically valid anyway.
#
# THE WORST CASE this covers is data/posts/*/content.html: agents/publisher
# replaces a live article's body_html with that file, so a conflict marker there
# becomes a conflict marker on a live indexed page.
data/** -merge
